{% doc %}
  blocks/_highlight-stage.liquid

  The photograph in the middle and the points pinned around it.

  ## The product owns a column, and that is the whole promise

  Above 990px the stage is three columns — points, product, points. A point is
  placed in column 1 or column 3 and the photograph is in column 2, so a label
  cannot end up over the product at any picture size. There is no arithmetic
  involved and therefore none to get wrong.

  It was coordinates once: every point carried an Across and a Down measured
  against the stage, with the photograph on a layer of its own inside it.
  Nothing tied the two together, so a taller bottle or a wider setting put
  labels across the product and the only fix was for somebody to notice. A
  section cannot promise a clear product that way.

  **Gap from the product** on each point is a margin on the side facing the
  middle, so it only ever moves a point further out — which is what gives each
  row of the drawing its outward stagger, with one number rather than a left
  value and a right value that have to be kept equal.

  ## Two layouts, not one layout scaled

  Below 990px there is no room either side of a photograph for a column to
  mean anything. So the placement is not scaled down, it is dropped:
  `assets/base.css` gives the picture a row of its own and the points follow it
  in source order, two across on a tablet and one on a phone.

  That is a different layout on purpose. Squeezing three columns into 390px
  gives five labels fighting a photograph and a merchant who cannot fix it,
  because the thing that is wrong is the idea, not the numbers.

  ## The light is an attribute, and Liquid writes the first one

  `<highlight-points>` in `assets/highlight-points.js` advances
  `data-highlight-active` along the points as the stage crosses the viewport.
  It is the shared scroll ticker from `assets/motion-engine.js` — the same one
  the parallax backgrounds use — rather than a listener of this section's own.

  The element is asked for by `data-activate="scroll"`. Switch **Light the
  points as the section scrolls** off and it stands down entirely, leaving the
  point the merchant marked lit. Without the module at all the same thing
  happens, which is why the state is an attribute in the markup rather than
  something the script creates.

  ## What the active point becomes lives here, not on each point

  Fill, glyph colour and ring colour for the lit state are the stage's
  settings, because "the lit one is filled green" is a property of the set. Set
  on each point they are five places for one decision to drift, which is the
  same reasoning `blocks/_featured-pills-tabs.liquid` gives for its own marks.

  **When a point lights up** is here for the same reason. Those colours are the
  state the light leaves behind; this is the movement it arrives with — a halo
  opening out of the mark once, or the mark breathing while it holds — and one
  run cannot arrive two different ways.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  comment
    ---- the picture ----

    `snippets/image.liquid` renders it, so it is responsive, lazily loaded and
    never a layout shift — and it draws its own placeholder, which is what an
    unfilled stage in the editor should show: a picture-shaped box the merchant
    can place points against, rather than empty space they cannot aim at.
  endcomment
  assign ratio = s.image_ratio | default: 'aspect-ratio'

  comment
    The middle column's width. Widening it narrows the two the points are in
    rather than growing the picture over them, which is the difference between
    this and the layered version it replaced.
  endcomment
  assign media_width = s.media_width | default: 28 | plus: 0
  assign media_width_tablet = s.media_width_tablet | default: 46 | plus: 0
  assign media_width_mobile = s.media_width_mobile | default: 62 | plus: 0

  assign min_height = s.min_height | default: 560 | plus: 0
  assign min_height_mobile = s.min_height_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0

  comment
    Which grid row the product starts on, and therefore which rows are the
    ones above it. Column 2 is the product's from here to the bottom of the
    stage, so a centre point belongs on a row before this number — that is
    the whole of the rule that keeps a label off the picture.
  endcomment
  comment
    How many rows the stage has.

    It has to be stated rather than left to the browser: the product is
    placed with `grid-row: <start> / -1`, and -1 is the last line of the
    *explicit* grid. Without a track list there is no explicit grid, -1
    resolves to line 1, and the product lands on the row above instead of
    the rows below — on top of the very point it is supposed to sit under.
  endcomment
  assign rows = s.rows | default: 3 | plus: 0

  assign media_row = s.media_row | default: 2 | plus: 0

  assign columns_tablet = s.columns_tablet | default: 2 | plus: 0
  assign columns_mobile = s.columns_mobile | default: 1 | plus: 0

  comment
    Presence, not a value. `data-activate` is what the component tests, so the
    off state has to emit a different string rather than a falsy one — an
    attribute that says `false` is still an attribute.
  endcomment
  assign activate = 'none'
  if s.activate_on_scroll
    assign activate = 'scroll'
  endif

  assign scroll_window = s.scroll_window | default: 60 | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- what the light does when it lands ----

    The fill, the glyph colour and the size are a *state*: they cross once and
    stay, and they are already set here for the whole set. This is the movement
    laid over that, and it belongs here for the same reason — "the light arrives
    like this" is a property of the run, not of any one point, and five copies
    of it are five places for one decision to drift.

    An attribute rather than a custom property, because `assets/base.css` has to
    *select* on it: each style is a different keyframe on a different element,
    which is a rule to match and not a value to substitute. Same shape as
    `data-activate` above.
  endcomment
  assign active_motion = s.active_motion | default: 'halo'
  assign active_motion_duration = s.active_motion_duration | default: 900 | plus: 0
-%}

{%- capture stage_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  --hps-media-width: {{ media_width }}%;
  --hps-media-width-tablet: {{ media_width_tablet }}%;
  --hps-media-width-mobile: {{ media_width_mobile }}%;
  --hps-min-height: {{ min_height }}px;
  --hps-min-height-mobile: {{ min_height_mobile }}px;
  --hps-rows: {{ rows }};
  --hps-media-row: {{ media_row }};
  --hps-columns-tablet: {{ columns_tablet }};
  --hps-columns-mobile: {{ columns_mobile }};
  --hps-gap: {{ s.gap | default: 24 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --hps-gap-mobile: {{ s.gap_mobile | default: 16 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- comment -%}
    What the lit point becomes. Each is emitted only when the merchant chose
    one, so a stage that has set none of them leaves every point drawing
    exactly what `snippets/icon-mark.liquid` gave it — the lit one included.
    `assets/base.css` reads these as overrides on the painted result rather
    than on the `--icon-mark-*` properties themselves, because those are inline
    on each mark and an inline custom property beats every stylesheet.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if s.active_fill != blank -%}
    --hps-active-fill: {{ s.active_fill }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.active_icon_color != blank -%}
    --hps-active-icon-color: {{ s.active_icon_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.active_ring_color != blank -%}
    --hps-active-ring-color: {{ s.active_ring_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  --hps-active-ring-opacity: {%- if s.active_keep_ring %}1{% else %}0{% endif -%};
  --hps-active-motion-duration: {{ active_motion_duration }}ms;
  {%- comment -%}
    The halo's own colour, and only when the merchant picked one. Left alone it
    falls to the lit ring colour and then to `currentColor` in the stylesheet,
    so a stage that recoloured the lit mark has recoloured the halo with it and
    there is never a second colour to keep in step.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if s.active_halo_color != blank -%}
    --hps-halo-color: {{ s.active_halo_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.active_scale != blank -%}
    --hps-active-scale: {{ s.active_scale | divided_by: 100.0 }};
  {%- endif -%}
  --hps-idle-opacity: {{ s.idle_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0 | divided_by: 100.0 }};
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The animation wraps the stage; the stage holds the picture and the points as
  siblings.

  Nothing may come between `<highlight-points>` and a point, because the points
  are grid items of the stage — the column that keeps them off the product is
  the stage's own, and a wrapper in the middle would be one grid item holding
  all five with no columns at all. So `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` is a direct
  child here, and the entrance animation is a `<motion-effect>` on the outside
  aiming at `.highlight-point` rather than a layer on the inside.

  `target` and not `children: true`: the wrapper's only child is the stage, so
  staggering its children would stagger one element. The points are what arrive
  one after another, so the points are what the stagger is pointed at.

  The outer wrapper does not disturb the scroll run either. `<highlight-points>`
  measures its own box against the viewport, and a wrapper that adds no padding
  of its own is the same box.
{%- endcomment -%}
<motion-effect
  class="highlight-stage-motion"
  {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', block: s, target: '.highlight-point' %}
>
  <highlight-points
    class="highlight-stage"
    data-activate="{{ activate }}"
    data-window="{{ scroll_window }}"
    data-active-motion="{{ active_motion }}"
    data-block-spacing
    style="{{ stage_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
    {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      `kind: 'image'` and not `'text'`: the subject is a photograph, and the
      text family's presets split their target into words and letters.

      This one *is* a grid item — column 2, from its own row to the bottom of
      the stage — so the wrapper is the picture's box and nothing is nested
      inside the cell that does not need to be.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <motion-effect
      class="highlight-stage__media"
      {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'image', block: s %}
    >
      {% render 'image',
         image: s.image,
         mobile_image: s.image_mobile,
         alt: s.alt,
         ratio: ratio,
         mobile_ratio: s.image_ratio_mobile,
         sizes: '(min-width: 990px) 24vw, 60vw',
         priority: s.priority,
         preload: s.priority,
         wrapper_class: 'highlight-stage__media-frame' %}
    </motion-effect>

    {% content_for 'blocks' %}
  </highlight-points>
</motion-effect>

{%- comment -%}
  Every setting below is a control a merchant reaches in the editor, and the
  schema is one element's styling surface: splitting it to get under Theme
  Check's count of 40 would split the thing the settings style.
{%- endcomment -%}
